aristotle-ethics-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aristotle-ethics-1.logic.r1 and aristotle-ethics-1.logic.r1.3 have "shame_physical → shame_feeling ∧ ¬shame_moral_state"; aristotle-ethics-1.logic.r1.2 has "shame_fear_disgrace ∧ shame_blush → shame_physical" and "shame_physical → shame_feeling ∧ ¬shame_moral_state"; they differ when only "Shame is defined as a kind of fear of disgrace" and "they who feel Shame grow red" are true, which the first allows
aristotle-ethics-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aristotle-ethics-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "Shame is defined as a kind of fear of disgrace" and "they who feel Shame grow red" are true and "Shame is in a way physical", "Shame is a feeling" and "Shame is a moral state" are false. Is S3, "So both are evidently in a way physical, which is thought to be a mark of a feeling rather than a moral state.", true then?
aristotle-ethics-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aristotle-ethics-1.logic.r1 has "shame_blush → shame_physical" and "shame_virtue → shame_moral_state"; aristotle-ethics-1.logic.r1.2 has "shame_virtue → shame_moral_state"; they differ when only "they who feel Shame grow red" and "Shame is a moral state" are true, which the second allows; aristotle-ethics-1.logic.r1.3 has "shame_fear_disgrace ∧ shame_blush → shame_physical" and "shame_virtue → shame_moral_state"
